#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
hex_length_filler.py
====================

Expand the 16-bit hex length field found in each Excel row into that many
filled output columns. Works as a desktop GUI or as a command-line tool.

Start
-----
    python hex_length_filler.py                    # GUI (also: double-click the file)
    python hex_length_filler.py --gui input.xlsx   # GUI with the input pre-filled
    python hex_length_filler.py input.xlsx [opts]  # command line, see --help

Input
-----
The first worksheet of an .xlsx / .xlsm workbook (or the sheet you choose).
Every row holds a hex byte sequence such as ``3D 03 10 2B``, either in one
cell or spread across several cells. Only the LAST TWO bytes of the row are
used. By default the first of them is the LSB and the second the MSB:

    3D 03 10 2B   ->  LSB 0x10, MSB 0x2B  ->  0x2B10 = 11024 columns
    ...   10 00   ->  0x0010 =    16 columns
    ...   FF FF   ->  0xFFFF = 65535 columns

Accepted byte spellings: ``10``, ``0x10``, single digits (``3`` -> 0x03) and
run-together pairs (``3D03102B``). Bytes may be separated by spaces, commas,
semicolons, colons, hyphens or pipes. Text that is not a hex byte is ignored
unless it lands in the last two positions, so a label in an earlier column
(e.g. a test-case ID) is harmless. If a comment column sits AFTER the bytes,
restrict the input columns (GUI "Read columns" / CLI --columns).

Output
------
Each output row gets ``length`` cells holding the fill value (default
``0x00``). Output row N belongs to the N-th processed input row; empty or
invalid input rows produce a blank output row so the alignment is kept. A
"Report" sheet lists every input row with its decoded LSB/MSB, length, output
location and status.

Excel column limit
------------------
An .xlsx sheet holds at most 16,384 columns (A..XFD), so FF FF (65,535) cannot
fit on one row of one sheet. Choose the behaviour (GUI "Long rows" / CLI
--overflow):

  split  (default)  Continue the SAME row number on extra sheets named
                    "Bytes 1-16384", "Bytes 16385-32768", ... Row N on every
                    data sheet still belongs to input row N.
  wrap              Continue on the following rows of the same sheet.
  error             Stop before writing anything.

A .csv output has no column limit (one input row = one CSV row of the full
length), but note that Excel only shows the first 16,384 columns of a CSV.

Runs fully offline: no network access, no cloud services, no telemetry.
Dependencies: openpyxl (pip install openpyxl); tkinter (bundled with the
standard Windows/macOS Python installers) for the GUI.

Exit codes (command line)
-------------------------
    0    success, every row converted
    1    fatal error, nothing written
    2    invalid command-line usage
    3    output written, but one or more input rows were invalid
    130  interrupted (Ctrl+C), nothing written
"""

